Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Smart Inventory Shows No Hosts
Symptoms: Smart inventory is created but shows 0 hosts

Solutions:

Verify host facts are up to date:

ansible all -i inventory -m setup
Check filter syntax:

Ensure proper Django query format
Verify fact names are correct
Test with simpler filters first
Validate fact availability:

Check if the facts you're filtering on exist
Use debug tasks to examine available facts
Issue 2: Filter Syntax Errors
Symptoms: Error messages when saving smart inventory

Solutions:

Common syntax corrections:

# Correct
ansible_facts.ansible_os_family == "RedHat"

# Incorrect
ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
Use proper operators:

== for equality
!= for inequality
>, <, >=, <= for comparisons
in for containment
and, or for logical operations
Issue 3: Hosts Not Updating Dynamically
Symptoms: Changes to hosts don't reflect in smart inventories

Solutions:

Force fact gathering:

ansible-playbook -i inventory gather_facts.yml
Check inventory sync settings

Verify AWX/Controller has proper permissions to access hosts

Advanced Smart Inventory Examples
Example 1: Compliance-Based Inventory
Name: Compliant-Servers
Description: Servers meeting security compliance requirements
Host Filter: ansible_facts.ansible_selinux.status == "enabled" and ansible_facts.ansible_memtotal_mb >= 4096 and "fail2ban" in ansible_facts.get('packages', {})
Example 2: Maintenance Window Inventory
Name: Maintenance-Window-Servers
Description: Servers available for maintenance based on load
Host Filter: ansible_facts.ansible_loadavg.1m < 2.0 and ansible_facts.ansible_memfree_mb > 512
Example 3: Geographic-Based Inventory
Name: East-Coast-Servers
Description: Servers in East Coast data centers
Host Filter: ansible_facts.ansible_hostname.startswith("east-") or ansible_facts.ansible_default_ipv4.address.startswith("10.1.")
Best Practices for Smart Inventories
Performance Optimization
Use Specific Filters

Avoid overly broad filters that match too many hosts
Use indexed fields when possible
Limit Complex Queries

Break complex filters into multiple smart inventories
Use simpler logic when possible
Maintenance and Management
Regular Review

Periodically review smart inventory filters
Update filters as infrastructure changes
Documentation

Document the purpose and logic of each smart inventory
Maintain a mapping of business requirements to filters
Testing

Test filters in development before production
Validate results regularly
